INT. SLEEPER TRAIN - DINING CAR - NIGHT

A narrow carriage, polished wood and brass, white tablecloths swaying with the rails. Outside the windows, black fields slide by in the dark, broken now and then by a lone farm light.

Five passengers sit scattered among the tables, each with a half-finished meal and a private worry.

DOCTOR MIRABELLE ACHEBE-LONG (50s, tailored suit, a leather bag at her feet) cuts a piece of fish with surgical care. Opposite her sits COLONEL PERCIVAL HOLLOWAY-GRANT (70s, regimental tie, a monocle) tapping a cigarette against a silver case. At the next table, MISS ODALYS FERNSBY (20s, hat with a feather, gloves) peers into a compact mirror. Across the aisle, a heavyset man, MR. VLADIMIR ZHURAVLEV (60s, fur collar, hands like shovels), pours vodka into a glass. And near the door, the porter, DESMOND ISAIAH (30s, white jacket, a tray), waits with the patience of the trained.

MIRABELLE
Colonel, you were saying about the Rhodesian campaign.

PERCIVAL
Was I? Yes. The heat was infernal.

ODALYS
(not looking up)
It must have been dreadful.

VLADIMIR
(over her)
War is always dreadful.

PERCIVAL
(over him)
I recall a particularly dreadful afternoon in nineteen eleven.

MIRABELLE
(to Odalys)
Miss Fernsby, are you quite well?

ODALYS
Perfectly.

VLADIMIR
She is not. Look at her hands.

ODALYS
My hands are fine.

DESMOND
Shall I bring tea, miss?

ODALYS
No, thank you.

PERCIVAL
Tea is a sovereign remedy.

MIRABELLE
So is silence.

Percival glares. Vladimir laughs, a bark. The train lurches around a curve and a glass falls from a nearby table, shattering. Everyone looks. Desmond bends to sweep it up.

DESMOND
Beg pardon, ladies and gentlemen.

PERCIVAL
Careful, my boy.

DESMOND
Yes, Colonel.

As he gathers the shards, he stops. In the debris lies a small brass key. He glances up. Odalys has gone very pale. Her eyes meet his. She shakes her head, once.

Desmond closes his hand over the key and says nothing. He stands. The train rushes on.
